# kubara bootstrap run again on the kind lab's hub after handover, recorded on
# 2026-09-30 with rebootstrap.sh, straight after the run in
# run-approve-2026-09-30.log on the same clusters (KIND=klab24,
# PREFIX=klab24-0930), with Kubara v0.16.0 and the same cub kubara build.
#
# Before it, every routed ApplicationSet read ConfigHub, and ConfigHub had
# released three metrics-server replicas to both clusters. The platform's Git
# repository still held the chart's default, one. The script took 4 minutes.
#
# What to look for:
# - Section 2: kubara bootstrap succeeds, and says nothing about ConfigHub.
# - Section 3: it has written Kubara's Git sources back into every routed
#   ApplicationSet, beside the ConfigHub source handover gave each one. Its
#   field manager, kubara-argocd-bootstrap, owns spec.template.spec.sources.
#   Argo CD reads sources before source, so every Application reads Git again,
#   and it synced metrics-server on the hub back to Git's one replica. It
#   changed nothing in ConfigHub.
# - cub kubara check fails each variant, and names the cause first: "it still
#   lists Git sources, which Argo CD reads before the release". Its prune
#   lists compare the release with what the Git sync left, so they are long.
# - Section 4: handover.sh again is the whole recovery. Steps 0 to 4 find
#   every release already published. Step 5 finds that nothing would be
#   pruned, applies the routed ApplicationSets, and removes the Git sources
#   again. Every Application reads ConfigHub, and metrics-server runs three
#   replicas again.
# - Straight after it, traefik was still OutOfSync while Argo CD synced it.
#   The check at the end, a minute later, shows what the lab shows after any
#   run on kind: cert-manager and the hub's argocd Degraded, traefik
#   Progressing, and every other variant passing.

